      "text": "To determine the domain of the function \\( u(x) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}} \\), we need to identify the values of \\( x \\) for which the function is defined. \n\n### Step 1: Analyze the Square Root Function\nThe expression inside the square root, \\( x \\), must be non-negative because the square root of a negative number is not defined in the set of real numbers. Therefore, we require:\n\\[ x \\geq 0 \\]\n\n### Step 2: Consider the Denominator\nThe denominator of the fraction \\( \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}} \\) is \\( \\sqrt{x} \\). The square root function \\( \\sqrt{x} \\) is defined for all \\( x \\geq 0 \\). Additionally, \\( \\sqrt{x} \\) is defined only when \\( x \\neq 0 \\). If \\( x = 0 \\), the term \\( \\sqrt{x} \\) is not defined (i.e., \\( \\sqrt{0} = 0 \\) and division by zero is undefined).\n\n### Step 3: Combine the Conditions\nFrom the above analysis, we conclude that \\( x \\) must be greater than or equal to 0 to ensure that both the square root and the denominator are defined. Therefore, the domain of the function \\( u(x) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}} \\) is:\n\\[ x \\in [0, \\infty) \\]\n\n### Final Answer\nThe domain of the function \\( u(x) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}} \\) in interval notation is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{[0, \\infty)}\n\\]",
